# CommunityOps console configuration
# Copy to .env.local and fill in. NEVER commit a real .env file.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Live mode (talks to the deployed AWS backend)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# All three values come from the CloudFormation stack outputs:
#   aws cloudformation describe-stacks --stack-name CommunityOps \
#     --region ap-south-1 --query "Stacks[0].Outputs"

VITE_API_URL=
VITE_COGNITO_USER_POOL_ID=
VITE_COGNITO_CLIENT_ID=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Federated sign-in (not available yet)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The sign-in screen renders one "Continue with …" button per provider named
# here, and nothing when the list is empty or absent — no disabled button, no
# decorative button, no block and no divider at all.
#
# Both values are needed before a single button appears, because a button is only
# rendered when it has somewhere real to go: the allowlist says which providers
# to offer, and the hosted UI domain is what the redirect is addressed to.
#
# Neither is set today. The user pool has SupportedIdentityProviders: [COGNITO],
# no UserPoolDomain, no OAuth flows, scopes or callback URLs and no identity
# provider resources, so there is nothing to turn on yet. Filling these in before
# that infrastructure exists changes nothing on screen, by design.
#
# Supported keys: google, amazon, apple. Provider client secrets belong in
# Secrets Manager and are referenced by template.yaml — never here, and never in
# the bundle.
# VITE_AUTH_PROVIDERS=google,apple
# VITE_COGNITO_DOMAIN=communityops.auth.ap-south-1.amazoncognito.com

# Fallback organization only. The console scopes requests to the organization it
# reads from the signed-in user's `cognito:groups` claim, so this value is used
# only when no session names one — before sign-in, or for an account that is in
# no organization group. Setting it grants nothing: the API authorizes every
# request against the caller's own groups and returns 403 otherwise.
VITE_ORG_ID=ORG-wemakedev

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Capability flags
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A capability flag states whether the backend contract behind an area of the
# product exists. It is not a permission: access is decided by API Gateway and
# tenancy.authorize_organization, never by the console.
#
# Off unless the value is exactly "true". While a flag is off, the navigation
# entry is absent and the route resolves to the not-found view.
#
# AttendeeOps needs GET /events/{eventId}/attendees and seeded attendee records,
# neither of which exists yet.
# VITE_CAPABILITY_ATTENDEE_OPS=true

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Try Demo Account (not available yet)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The sign-in screen renders a "Try Demo Account" action only when BOTH values
# below are set, and nothing at all when either is missing — no disabled button
# and no placeholder. A button that cannot authenticate is worse than no button.
#
# This is a real Cognito SRP sign-in as a preconfigured demo identity, not a
# bypass: the token, the authorizer and the tenancy check are the same ones every
# other session goes through, so the demo user reads only the demo organization.
#
# Neither is set today. There is no ORG-demo Cognito group, no demo identity and
# no demo organization seeded with its own event, so there is nothing for these
# values to name. Filling them in before that exists only produces a button whose
# every press fails.
#
# NEVER commit a real value here. Anything in the bundle is public, which is
# acceptable only for an identity whose entire reachable dataset is synthetic —
# and CI scans this repository for secrets. Set the real values in .env.local,
# and create the account with a script that reads the password from a file or a
# parameter, the way scripts/live-e2e-test.py already does.
# VITE_DEMO_USERNAME=demo@communityops.dev
# VITE_DEMO_PASSWORD=set-in-.env.local-never-here

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Demo mode (local UI work with no backend)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Not the same thing as "Try Demo Account" above, and the console keeps them
# apart on screen: this serves fabricated local records and shows an amber
# "Demo Mode" badge, while a demo *session* shows a neutral "Demo workspace" chip
# and every record in it is a real API response.
# Opt-in only. When true, the console serves built-in sample data and shows a
# "Demo Mode" badge. It is never used as a fallback for a failing backend.
# VITE_USE_MOCK=true
